[38;5;231m⏺[39m Structure is locked in. Part two — verification, which has one real decision in it.

  [1mThe honest constraint:[0m choosing browser-only (no UMD wrapper) is what makes this awkward to unit-test. [38;5;153mconfig.js[39m will
  assign to [38;5;153mwindow[39m as a side effect of loading, so Node can't just [38;5;153mrequire[39m it and call [38;5;153mdetectEnvironment()[39m. Testing it
  automatically means either a DOM environment (jsdom + a runner) or loading the file into a [38;5;153mnode:vm[39m sandbox with a fake
  [38;5;153mwindow[39m/[38;5;153mlocation[39m. That's a deliberate trade we already made for simplicity — I'm flagging the bill for it now rather
  than pretending it isn't there.

  [1mThe repo has no test infrastructure at all:[0m [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153mscripts[39m, no dependencies, and there are no test files.
  So this isn't "add a test to the existing suite" — it's "decide whether this change is the moment to start a suite."

  [1mManual verification either way[0m, since the browser is the only real runtime: open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly (hostname [38;5;153m""[39m →
  [38;5;153mdevelopment[39m, so [38;5;153mAppConfig.apiBaseUrl[39m should read [38;5;153mhttp://localhost:3000[39m), submit the form, and confirm the console
  shows the composed URL. Serving it from a non-localhost host should resolve to production.

  [1mMy recommendation: manual verification only, no test infra in this change.[0m The whole change is roughly twenty lines,
  and standing up a runner plus a DOM shim would be several times the size of the thing under test. If you want a suite,
  it deserves its own task where the goal is the harness, not a config move.
